{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head 73d6282c4bed94507a93b7a14dc94abdb28581a5, citing both artifacts.\n\nI traced the boundary rather than reading its description, because a rebinding guard that admits what it should refuse passes every test written against what it does.\n\nADMISSION IS GENUINELY CLOSED-SET AND RESOLVER-FREE. loopbackRequestHostWithResolver splits host and port and refuses if either is empty or the split errors; requires the port to parse as a base-10 unsigned 16-bit value and to be non-zero; then admits only ip.IsLoopback() for a literal IP, or localhost / localhost. compared case-insensitively. There is no fallback branch. The resolve parameter is declared and never called, and loopbackRequestHost passes nil, so no resolver exists on the admission path at all rather than merely being unused.\n\nTHE GUARD IS IN FORCE, WHICH IS A SEPARATE FACT FROM BEING CORRECT. TrustedHostHandler wraps server.Handler() — the whole mux, not a route subset — at cmd/gs/main.go:2238, which is the single production assembly of the resident HTTP server.\n\nI CHASED ONE SUSPECTED BYPASS TO A NEGATIVE AND AM RECORDING IT. internal/perfscenario/run.go calls server.Handler() at five sites with no TrustedHostHandler wrapper. That matters more than it looks: DNS rebinding specifically targets loopback-bound services, so a second unguarded listener would reopen exactly what this head closes. It is not one. perfscenario drives the handler through httptest.NewRequest and httptest.NewRecorder, in-process, and binds no listener — the only ListenAndServe-shaped construction in non-test code is the guarded one at cmd/gs/main.go:2274. No exposure.\n\nI am recording the negative because \"no defect, inspected\" is worth more to a later reader than silence, and because if anyone later gives perfscenario a real listener this is the note that says why it broke.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME, NOT QUOTED. I reintroduced resolver-based admission — after the localhost check, call resolve(host) and admit if any returned address is loopback — and TestLoopbackRequestHostRefusesDNSNameWithoutResolver failed with \"ordinary DNS name resolving to loopback was admitted\". internal/service/server.go returned to its baseline sha256 7809c0ad8b7c70fce92aefae23ccf7f00cfc9fe963cbdcaa980eab18d427d7d9 afterwards and the checkout was clean.\n\nThat test is well built for this job: it asserts both halves independently — that the DNS name is refused, and that resolverCalls is zero. A guard could pass the first by accident; only the second pins the policy.\n\nDOCUMENTATION FIDELITY, CHECKED CLAUSE BY CLAUSE AGAINST THE ENFORCEMENT SITE rather than read for plausibility. architecture.md says the resident \"requires the Host to contain an explicit numeric port and either a literal loopback IP or localhost, compared without case and with one optional trailing dot. It never resolves request hostnames.\" Every clause matches: explicit numeric port is the ParseUint with a non-zero check; literal loopback IP is ip.IsLoopback(); without case is EqualFold; one optional trailing dot is the literal \"localhost.\" arm, one dot and not arbitrary trailing dots; never resolves is the never-called seam. This is unusually precise for a security note and I could not find a place where it overstates what the code does.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This changes only the Layer 6 resident HTTP surface: one handler wrapping the mux, and one predicate. No kernel, application or fold contract is touched, and docs/reference/architecture.md records the boundary in the same head, so the contract change is published rather than implied.\n\nSECURITY. Examined the trust boundary end to end, as above: closed-set admission with no resolver reachable, the guard applied to the whole mux at the only production listener, no second serving path, malformed and portless values refused (the fixture list includes \"\", \"localhost\", \"127.0.0.1\", \"::1\", \"localhost:\"), bracketed IPv6 admitted via SplitHostPort plus ParseIP, and the refusal path emitting 400 with no-store rather than leaking. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The resolver seam is the one piece that could be called unnecessary machinery, and it is not: it exists solely so a test can distinguish this policy from resolver-based admission, and the mutation above shows it earns its place. I identified nothing to remove that would not weaken a condition.\n\nGATES, run by me in a clean detached checkout at the exact head: gofmt -l silent, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check clean, and one uncached go test -count=1 ./... run, 32 packages ok, zero failures, internal/service passing at 57.835s. I ran one full suite rather than two here, which is what \"proportionate\" means for a review of this size; for heads I implement myself I run two, and I would rather state the difference than let the word cover it.\n\nONE THING I AM RAISING RATHER THAN BLOCKING ON. 5b4b3c29 stands at docs/reference/architecture.md, and a merge into main published c608929e at that same path shortly before this review. Whether those collide is a merge-time question for codex and its preflight, not a defect in this head, but planner has been sequencing that path deliberately and I would rather name it here than have it surface when the merge is attempted.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2b3a3252488be0e573f467b90096463dc16b208","head":"73d6282c4bed94507a93b7a14dc94abdb28581a5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0d6cf2f55a393fdf0f519352b5d5e94502995cf3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4690aa02a69039c85eb25c2e673e9253debf2270","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}